On May 16, 2:25 pm, purple arcanist <ejg_...@yahoo.com> wrote:
> 1. Eliminate the death drop.  This eliminates the farming of monsters
> to get loot, and stops wierd loot combinations.  It is just wrong when
> you kill something and it drops something wierd.  A wolf has a wand of
> wishing.  How did it get it? Did the wizard of yendor decide to hide
> it in the wolf's mouth?
>
Without deathdrops, there are only finitely many items in the game
besides randomly generated monster equipment. That pins way, *way* too
much of a game on random luck. As it is, Nethack successfully avoids
the problems of overleveling (by making items far more important than
level) and overemphasis on luck, in part via death drops. You get
corpses from monsters. You get items from monsters. You sacrifice
monsters to obtain artifacts.

As for realism, too much realism ruins a game. It's quite "realistic"
that a random gnome might dabble in magic and carry a magic marker
around, or maybe some kobold wants to trick his boss into reading a
scroll of amnesia, etc., but Nethack can only track so much
information. Since tracking the information needed to make item drops
"realistic" doesn't significantly affect gameplay, there's no real
reason to avoid random death drops (besides the canonical Pudding
Farming Problem, but that's another issue).

> 2. Revise prayer.  One of the only things prayer can do is fix your
> troubles.  The only other possible thing that prayer can do is get you
> crowned.  Nothing else.  Prayer benefits are way too cheesy.
> Sacrificing will not get you artifacts, but it will have the normal
> effects.
>

Um, I'm not exactly sure what you're trying to communicate. Getting a
divinely blessed pointy stick sure doesn't seem cheesy to me. ;) If
you're talking about the castle passtune, I'd agree that it's not that
great a benefit in Vanilla nethack. I'd much rather have the level of
Vlad's tower or the Castle for levelport/searching purposes.

Sporkhack revises prayer somewhat by giving low level characters
blessed fixed +5 armor/weapons rather than artifacts. Is it a bit too
powerful? Maybe, but the *monsters* are way more powerful too, to the
extent that you need to be skilled in your weapon/in two weapon combat
with high DEX to even *hit* Asmodeus.

> 3. Revise spellcasting.  Some spells are either too low level or
> useless.  Some of these include:
>
> Too powerful: polymorph(ban, and make it wand/potion only), magic
> missile, fireball, cone of cold(cap the damage lower), identify (needs
> to be higher level), sleep(monsters should wake up when attacked, or
> sleep should be higher level)
>
> Weak spells: cure blindness, cure sickness (sort of), restore ability

I might agree that some of the attack spells are too powerful. But a
player needs damn good incentive to train to "skilled" in attack
spells, increase PW to an acceptable spellcasting value, and invest in
special equipment such as a Helm of Brilliance. For non-wizards, the
effort required is much, much greater than the effort required to
enchant and swing around an artifact weapon.

For those reasons, Cone of Cold and Polymorph are near the bottom of
my list of broken spells. Charm monster is ridiculously broken. Sleep
could certainly stand to be level 2, but would require a few tweaks to
remain a Monk starting spell. Restore ability is admittedly crap since
it's only needed in the early game, when it's too difficult to cast,
but the unihorn is more broken than Restore Ability. Derek Ray removed
the stat cure ability of the unihorn in sporkhack and made Restore
Ability level 2 with good results.


